# Research state

Updated 6 November 2026, cycle 28. Working source: ../research_proposal_main.tex. Title: **When Test-Time Consistency Changes Predictions**. Author Amit Arad, advisor Assaf Shocher, acknowledgment Shoham Grunblat. Existing twice-daily automation.

## Decision and ranked directions

1. **Adviser review.** Recommend the scoped thesis on attribution of IT3 updates and their measured predictive consequences. The toy optimizer analyses remain thesis controls. Close the current recovery-paper candidate. Kang and Lee already vary Adam moment policies and learning-rate adjustment; PeTTA already compares recurring-domain resets. Neither inspected comparison isolates the reciprocal domain-specific return-risk contrast. The current evidence supplies neither a positive prediction beyond recent gradients nor a qualified independent compatible stream. See notes/2026-11-05-cycle25/Adviser_Decision.md.
2. **Completed compatible-task baseline.** With protected rows alone, every update changing current predictions has unbounded worst-case excess squared error under the unrestricted shared linear model. With a valid compact convex feasible coefficient set, projection gives the attained minimax gain. A bounded extra source reading produces a positive example. SAFER already contains the projection/optimality principle. Close this rule as a stand-alone paper and keep its information accounting in the thesis.
3. **Completed IT3 case study.** The two-update diagnostic verified a gradient difference but failed the registered accuracy criterion. Its sign identity is closed as an independent paper claim. Moving-reference stability, PEIRA and the prior finite-time failures remain supporting evidence.

## Next action

The scalar diagnostic analysis is complete. Output-only summaries, even including the refreshed value, leave the second connected gradient unidentified. Before suggesting an implementation advantage, run one data-free conformance and cost check on the audited ReLU architecture with deterministic synthetic inputs, zero training, and a 60-CPU-second cap. Compare direct gradients with the region method on certified cases and record abstentions. Any mismatch rejects implementation. Distinguish numerical agreement from a floating-point certificate, and synthetic coverage from trained coverage. Detailed limits and protocol are in notes/2026-11-06-cycle28/Summary_Result.md.

